  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 254 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nSunday through Friday:\n\nAs low pressure develops in the northern Plains Sunday, WAA and\nsouthwesterly winds will strengthen across southern Wisconsin. \nThis will bring highs in the mid to upper 80s and heat index\nvalues in the upper 80s to low 90s as upper level WAA driven \nprecipitation tapers off into midday. CAPE re-enters the \nforecast (1500-2000 J/kg) into the afternoon hours as the \nsurface front lifts into central Wisconsin. Capping inversion \nmay linger through much of the day, with storms tending to form \nalong the warm front itself in central Wisconsin. However, still\nsome uncertainty with the exact placement of the warm frontal\nboundary, so kept NBM PoPs of 15-35% through the day. A few of\nthe afternoon storms may become severe, but confidence remains\nlow. \n\nNext chances for precipitation return later in the overnight \nhours into Monday morning as the 850 mb southwesterly jet noses\ninto southwestern Wisconsin, enhancing thunderstorm development\nand bringing additional instability (MUCAPE 1000-1500 J/kg) to \neastern Iowa and southwestern Wisconsin. Any clusters that\ndevelop upstream in eastern Iowa would be pushed eastward into\nsouthern Wisconsin, with an additional chance for a few strong\nto severe storms.\n\nUpper level ridging remains over southern Wisconsin through the\nweek, with highs in the mid to upper 80s each day Monday through\nFriday. High humidity will keep heat index values in the\nlow to mid 90s. Off and on periods of thunderstorms are\npossible as ridge-rider complexes propagate through the mean\nflow. A few of these may become severe, but timing and intensity\nwill remain in question until the timeframe gets closer.\n\nMH",
